WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: Joanna Pietrulewicz

00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:17.934
So I'd like to start, if I may,
by asking you some questions.

00:00:17.958 --> 00:00:21.684
If you've ever lost someone
you truly love,

00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:23.684
ever had your heart broken,

00:00:23.708 --> 00:00:27.184
ever struggled through
an acrimonious divorce,

00:00:27.208 --> 00:00:30.059
or been the victim of infidelity,

00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:31.893
please stand up.

00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:35.851
If standing up isn't accessible to you,
you can put your hand up.

00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:37.768
Please, stay standing,

00:00:37.792 --> 00:00:40.143
and keep your hand up there.

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.434
If you've ever lived
through a natural disaster,

00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:44.476
been bullied or been made redundant,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.851
stand on up.

00:00:45.875 --> 00:00:49.101
If you've ever had a miscarriage,

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:50.934
if you've ever had an abortion

00:00:50.958 --> 00:00:53.893
or struggled through infertility,

00:00:53.917 --> 00:00:55.518
please stand up.

00:00:55.542 --> 00:00:58.934
Finally, if you, or anyone you love,

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.643
has had to cope
with mental illness, dementia,

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.143
some form of physical impairment,

00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.643
or cope with suicide,

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.101
please stand up.

00:01:09.125 --> 00:01:10.976
Look around you.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.268
Adversity doesn't discriminate.

00:01:16.292 --> 00:01:18.184
If you are alive,

00:01:18.208 --> 00:01:22.059
you are going to have to,
or you've already had to,

00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:24.684
deal with some tough times.

00:01:24.708 --> 00:01:26.917
Thank you, everyone, take a seat.

00:01:30.708 --> 00:01:34.559
I started studying
resilience research a decade ago,

00:01:34.583 --> 00:01:37.934
at the University
of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia.

00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:40.309
It was an amazing time to be there,

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.726
because the professors who trained me

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:49.143
had just picked up the contract
to train all 1.1 million American soldiers

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:53.768
to be as mentally fit
as they always have been physically fit.

00:01:53.792 --> 00:01:55.059
As you can imagine,

00:01:55.083 --> 00:01:59.184
you don't get a much more skeptical
discerning audience

00:01:59.208 --> 00:02:03.059
than the American drill sergeants
returning from Afganistan.

00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:04.351
So for someone like me,

00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:07.893
whose main quest in life
is trying to work out

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:12.768
how we take the best
of scientific findings out of academia

00:02:12.792 --> 00:02:15.559
and bring them to people
in their everyday lives,

00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:19.184
it was a pretty inspiring place to be.

00:02:19.208 --> 00:02:21.393
I finished my studies in America,

00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:24.018
and I returned home here to Christchurch

00:02:24.042 --> 00:02:27.351
to start my doctoral research.

00:02:27.375 --> 00:02:29.726
I'd just begun that study

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.726
when the Christchurch earthquakes hit.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.726
So I put my research on hold,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.434
and I started working
with my home community

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:43.059
to help them through that terrible
post-quake period.

00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:45.059
I worked with all sorts of organizations

00:02:45.083 --> 00:02:48.518
from government departments
to building companies,

00:02:48.542 --> 00:02:50.351
and all sorts of community groups,

00:02:50.375 --> 00:02:53.601
teaching them the ways
of thinking and acting

00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:56.684
that we know boost resilience.

00:02:56.708 --> 00:02:59.643
I thought that was my calling.

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:05.101
My moment to put all
of that research to good use.

00:03:05.125 --> 00:03:07.934
But sadly, I was wrong.

00:03:07.958 --> 00:03:12.934
For my own true test came in 2014

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:15.351
on Queen's Birthday weekend.

00:03:15.375 --> 00:03:18.143
We and two other families had decided

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:22.059
to go down to Lake Ohau
and bike the outs to ocean.

00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:24.101
At the last minute,

00:03:24.125 --> 00:03:27.184
my beautiful 12-year-old daughter Abi

00:03:27.208 --> 00:03:32.101
decided to hop in the car
with her best friend, Ella, also 12,

00:03:32.125 --> 00:03:35.625
and Ella's mom, Sally,
a dear, dear friend of mine.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.893
On the way down,
as they traveled through Rakaia

00:03:39.917 --> 00:03:41.601
on Thompsons Track,

00:03:41.625 --> 00:03:45.184
a car sped through a stop sign,

00:03:45.208 --> 00:03:46.893
crashing into them

00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:49.583
and killing all three of them instantly.

00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:53.518
In the blink of an eye,

00:03:53.542 --> 00:03:57.226
I find myself flung
to the other side of the equation,

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:00.309
waking up with a whole new identity.

00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:02.601
Instead of being the resilience expert,

00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:05.684
suddenly, I'm the grieving mother.

00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:08.184
Waking up not knowing who I am,

00:04:08.208 --> 00:04:11.518
trying to wrap my head
around unthinkable news,

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:14.559
my world smashed to smithereens.

00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:19.434
Suddenly, I'm the one on the end
of all this expert advice.

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:20.768
And I can tell you,

00:04:20.792 --> 00:04:24.268
I didn't like what I heard one little bit.

00:04:24.292 --> 00:04:26.518
In the days after Abi died,

00:04:26.542 --> 00:04:32.351
we were told we were now
prime candidates for family estrangement.

00:04:32.375 --> 00:04:34.643
That we were likely to get divorced

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:37.559
and we were at high risk
of mental illness.

00:04:37.583 --> 00:04:39.059
"Wow," I remember thinking,

00:04:39.083 --> 00:04:42.143
"Thanks for that, I though
my life was already pretty shit."

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:43.893
(Laughter)

00:04:43.917 --> 00:04:47.018
Leaflets described
the five stages of grief:

00:04:47.042 --> 00:04:50.309
anger, bargaining, denial,
depression, acceptance.

00:04:50.333 --> 00:04:52.684
Victim support arrived at our door

00:04:52.708 --> 00:04:58.375
and told us that we could expect
to write off the next five years to grief.

00:04:59.292 --> 00:05:03.309
I know the leaflets
and the resources meant well.

00:05:03.333 --> 00:05:04.934
But in all of that advice,

00:05:04.958 --> 00:05:08.559
they left us feeling like victims.

00:05:08.583 --> 00:05:11.143
Totally overwhelmed by the journey ahead,

00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:16.601
and powerless to exert any influence
over our grieving whatsoever.

00:05:16.625 --> 00:05:20.768
I didn't need to be told
how bad things were.

00:05:20.792 --> 00:05:24.768
Believe me, I already knew
things were truly terrible.

00:05:24.792 --> 00:05:27.875
What I needed most was hope.

00:05:28.708 --> 00:05:33.309
I needed a journey
through all that anguish,

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:36.101
pain and longing.

00:05:36.125 --> 00:05:37.393
Most of all,

00:05:37.417 --> 00:05:43.143
I wanted to be an active participant
in my grief process.

00:05:43.167 --> 00:05:46.351
So I decided to turn my back
on their advice

00:05:46.375 --> 00:05:50.768
and decided instead to conduct
something of a self-experiment.

00:05:50.792 --> 00:05:52.893
I'd done the research, I had the tools,

00:05:52.917 --> 00:05:56.059
I wanted to know how useful
they would be to me now

00:05:56.083 --> 00:06:00.059
in the face of such an enormous
mountain to climb.

00:06:00.083 --> 00:06:02.601
Now, I have to confess at this point,

00:06:02.625 --> 00:06:06.143
I didn't really know
that any of this was going to work.

00:06:06.167 --> 00:06:08.601
Parental bereavement
is widely acknowledged

00:06:08.625 --> 00:06:12.000
as the hardest of losses to bear.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.893
But I can tell you now, five years on,

00:06:16.917 --> 00:06:19.518
what I already knew from the research.

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:23.018
That you can rise up from adversity,

00:06:23.042 --> 00:06:25.518
that there are strategies that work,

00:06:25.542 --> 00:06:27.934
that it is utterly possible

00:06:27.958 --> 00:06:33.059
to make yourself think
and act in certain ways

00:06:33.083 --> 00:06:37.976
that help you navigate tough times.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.434
There is a monumental body of research
on how to do this stuff.

00:06:42.458 --> 00:06:45.893
Today, I'm just going to share
with you three strategies.

00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:49.768
These are my go-to strategies
that I relied upon

00:06:49.792 --> 00:06:52.393
and saved me in my darkest days.

00:06:52.417 --> 00:06:56.226
They're three strategies
that underpin all of my work,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.226
and they're pretty readily
available to us all,

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.601
anyone can learn them,

00:07:00.625 --> 00:07:03.000
you can learn them right here today.

00:07:04.083 --> 00:07:05.559
So number one,

00:07:05.583 --> 00:07:10.143
resilient people get that shit happens.

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:13.934
They know that suffering is part of life.

00:07:13.958 --> 00:07:16.143
This doesn't mean
they actually welcome it in,

00:07:16.167 --> 00:07:18.018
they're not actually delusional.

00:07:18.042 --> 00:07:21.184
Just that when the tough times come,

00:07:21.208 --> 00:07:22.934
they seem to know

00:07:22.958 --> 00:07:27.893
that suffering is part
of every human existence.

00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:32.434
And knowing this stops you
from feeling discriminated against

00:07:32.458 --> 00:07:34.559
when the tough times come.

00:07:34.583 --> 00:07:36.809
Never once did I find myself thinking,

00:07:36.833 --> 00:07:38.518
"Why me?"

00:07:38.542 --> 00:07:40.309
In fact, I remember thinking,

00:07:40.333 --> 00:07:42.268
"Why not me?

00:07:42.292 --> 00:07:43.809
Terrible things happen to you,

00:07:43.833 --> 00:07:45.934
just like they do everybody else.

00:07:45.958 --> 00:07:47.393
That's your life now,

00:07:47.417 --> 00:07:49.809
time to sink or swim."

00:07:49.833 --> 00:07:51.518
The real tragedy

00:07:51.542 --> 00:07:55.226
is that not enough of us
seem to know this any longer.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:56.518
We seem to live in an age

00:07:56.542 --> 00:07:58.684
where we're entitled to a perfect life,

00:07:58.708 --> 00:08:02.393
where shiny, happy photos
on Instagram are the norm,

00:08:02.417 --> 00:08:04.101
when actually,

00:08:04.125 --> 00:08:08.434
as you all demonstrated
at the start of my talk,

00:08:08.458 --> 00:08:11.250
the very opposite is true.

00:08:12.417 --> 00:08:14.268
Number two,

00:08:14.292 --> 00:08:15.875
resilient people

00:08:17.458 --> 00:08:23.792
are really good at choosing carefully
where they select their attention.

00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:28.559
They have a habit of realistically
appraising situations,

00:08:28.583 --> 00:08:33.434
and typically, managing to focus
on the things that they can change,

00:08:33.458 --> 00:08:38.851
and somehow accept
the things that they can't.

00:08:38.875 --> 00:08:44.875
This is a vital, learnable
skill for resilience.

00:08:46.042 --> 00:08:49.393
As humans, we are really good

00:08:49.417 --> 00:08:53.018
at noticing threats and weaknesses.

00:08:53.042 --> 00:08:56.976
We are hardwired for that negative.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.143
We're really, really good
at noticing them.

00:09:00.167 --> 00:09:05.101
Negative emotions stick to us like Velcro,

00:09:05.125 --> 00:09:10.434
whereas positive emotions and experiences
seems to bounce off like Teflon.

00:09:10.458 --> 00:09:13.893
Being wired in this way
is actually really good for us,

00:09:13.917 --> 00:09:17.518
and served us well
from an evolutionary perspective.

00:09:17.542 --> 00:09:19.726
So imagine for a moment I'm a cavewoman,

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.101
and I'm coming out
of my cave in the morning,

00:09:22.125 --> 00:09:24.268
and there's a saber-toothed
tiger on one side

00:09:24.292 --> 00:09:26.643
and a beautiful rainbow on the other.

00:09:26.667 --> 00:09:31.851
It kind of pays for my survival
for me to notice this tiger.

00:09:31.875 --> 00:09:33.143
The problem is,

00:09:33.167 --> 00:09:37.351
we now live in an era
where we are constantly bombarded

00:09:37.375 --> 00:09:39.643
by threats all day long,

00:09:39.667 --> 00:09:44.684
and our poor brains treat
every single one of those threats

00:09:44.708 --> 00:09:47.518
as though they were a tiger.

00:09:47.542 --> 00:09:51.018
Our threat focus, our stress response,

00:09:51.042 --> 00:09:54.809
is permanently dialed up.

00:09:54.833 --> 00:09:58.226
Resilient people
don't diminish the negative,

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:01.101
but they also have worked out a way

00:10:01.125 --> 00:10:04.208
of tuning into the good.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:09.559
One day, when doubts
were threatening to overwhelm me,

00:10:09.583 --> 00:10:11.393
I distinctly remember thinking,

00:10:11.417 --> 00:10:16.559
"No, you do not get
to get swallowed up by this.

00:10:16.583 --> 00:10:18.351
You have to survive.

00:10:18.375 --> 00:10:21.018
You've got so much to live for.

00:10:21.042 --> 00:10:24.101
Choose life, not death.

00:10:24.125 --> 00:10:26.684
Don't lose what you have

00:10:26.708 --> 00:10:29.143
to what you have lost."

00:10:29.167 --> 00:10:31.601
In psychology,
we call this benefit finding.

00:10:31.625 --> 00:10:33.351
In my brave new world,

00:10:33.375 --> 00:10:37.059
it involved trying to find things
to be grateful for.

00:10:37.083 --> 00:10:38.809
At least our wee girl

00:10:38.833 --> 00:10:43.101
hadn't died of some terrible,
long, drawn-out illness.

00:10:43.125 --> 00:10:45.309
She died suddenly, instantly,

00:10:45.333 --> 00:10:49.059
sparing us and her that pain.

00:10:49.083 --> 00:10:52.601
We had a huge amount of social support
from family and friends

00:10:52.625 --> 00:10:54.393
to help us through.

00:10:54.417 --> 00:10:55.851
And most of all,

00:10:55.875 --> 00:10:59.268
we still had two beautiful
boys to live for,

00:10:59.292 --> 00:11:00.976
who needed us now,

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:06.875
and deserved to have as normal a life
as we could possibly give them.

00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:11.143
Being able to switch the focus
of your attention

00:11:11.167 --> 00:11:13.101
to also include the good

00:11:13.125 --> 00:11:17.059
has been shown by science
to be a really powerful strategy.

00:11:17.083 --> 00:11:21.893
So in 2005, Martin Seligman and colleagues
conducted an experiment.

00:11:21.917 --> 00:11:26.518
And they asked people,
all they asked people to do,

00:11:26.542 --> 00:11:31.143
was think of three good things
that had happened to them each day.

00:11:31.167 --> 00:11:34.768
What they found, over the six months
course of this study,

00:11:34.792 --> 00:11:38.309
was that those people
showed higher levels of gratitude,

00:11:38.333 --> 00:11:39.976
higher levels of happiness

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.309
and less depression
over the course of the six-month study.

00:11:45.333 --> 00:11:46.857
When you're going through grief,

00:11:46.881 --> 00:11:49.351
you might need a reminder,

00:11:49.375 --> 00:11:52.476
or you might need permission
to feel grateful.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.268
In our kitchen, we've got
a bright pink neon poster

00:11:55.292 --> 00:11:58.726
that reminds us to "accept" the good.

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:00.518
In the American army,

00:12:00.542 --> 00:12:02.601
they framed it a little bit differently.

00:12:02.625 --> 00:12:06.809
They talked to the army
about hunting the good stuff.

00:12:06.833 --> 00:12:08.726
Find the language that works for you,

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:10.059
but whatever you do,

00:12:10.083 --> 00:12:14.393
make an intentional,
deliberate, ongoing effort

00:12:14.417 --> 00:12:17.333
to tune into what's good in your world.

00:12:18.292 --> 00:12:19.559
Number three,

00:12:19.583 --> 00:12:21.518
resilient people ask themselves,

00:12:21.542 --> 00:12:25.434
"Is what I'm doing helping or harming me?"

00:12:25.458 --> 00:12:29.351
This is a question that's used
a lot in good therapy.

00:12:29.375 --> 00:12:31.809
And boy, is it powerful.

00:12:31.833 --> 00:12:34.809
This was my go-to question

00:12:34.833 --> 00:12:37.226
in the days after the girls died.

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:40.976
I would ask it again and again.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.018
"Should I go to the trial
and see the driver?

00:12:44.042 --> 00:12:46.768
Would that help me or would it harm me?"

00:12:46.792 --> 00:12:48.684
Well, that was a no-brainer for me,

00:12:48.708 --> 00:12:50.559
I chose to stay away.

00:12:50.583 --> 00:12:53.309
But Trevor, my husband,
decided to meet with the driver

00:12:53.333 --> 00:12:55.101
at a later time.

00:12:55.125 --> 00:13:00.476
Late at night, I'd find myself sometimes
poring over old photos of Abi,

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:02.476
getting more and more upset.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:03.768
I'd ask myself,

00:13:03.792 --> 00:13:07.476
"Really? Is this helping you
or is it harming you?

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.143
Put away the photos,

00:13:09.167 --> 00:13:10.768
go to bed for the night,

00:13:10.792 --> 00:13:12.792
be kind to yourself."

00:13:13.833 --> 00:13:17.643
This question can be applied
to so many different contexts.

00:13:17.667 --> 00:13:21.684
Is the way I'm thinking and acting
helping or harming you,

00:13:21.708 --> 00:13:24.809
in your bid to get that promotion,

00:13:24.833 --> 00:13:26.434
to pass that exam,

00:13:26.458 --> 00:13:29.018
to recover from a heart attack?

00:13:29.042 --> 00:13:31.601
So many different ways.

00:13:31.625 --> 00:13:33.351
I write a lot about resilience,

00:13:33.375 --> 00:13:36.184
and over the years, this one strategy

00:13:36.208 --> 00:13:39.768
has prompted more positive
feedback than any other.

00:13:39.792 --> 00:13:42.143
I get scores of letters
and emails and things

00:13:42.167 --> 00:13:44.143
from all over the place of people saying

00:13:44.167 --> 00:13:47.101
what a huge impact
it's had on their lives.

00:13:47.125 --> 00:13:52.434
Whether it is forgiving family
ancient transgressions, arguments

00:13:52.458 --> 00:13:54.393
from Christmases past,

00:13:54.417 --> 00:13:57.809
or whether it is just
trolling through social media,

00:13:57.833 --> 00:14:00.184
whether it is asking yourself

00:14:00.208 --> 00:14:03.542
whether you really need
that extra glass of wine.

00:14:04.875 --> 00:14:08.934
Asking yourself whether what you're doing,
the way you're thinking,

00:14:08.958 --> 00:14:10.226
the way you're acting

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:13.184
is helping or harming you,

00:14:13.208 --> 00:14:16.101
puts you back in the driver's seat.

00:14:16.125 --> 00:14:21.000
It gives you some control
over your decision-making.

00:14:22.792 --> 00:14:24.643
Three strategies.

00:14:24.667 --> 00:14:25.917
Pretty simple.

00:14:26.875 --> 00:14:29.476
They're readily available to us all,

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:32.351
anytime, anywhere.

00:14:32.375 --> 00:14:35.643
They don't require rocket science.

00:14:35.667 --> 00:14:39.059
Resilience isn't some fixed trait.

00:14:39.083 --> 00:14:40.351
It's not elusive,

00:14:40.375 --> 00:14:43.292
that some people have
and some people don't.

00:14:44.125 --> 00:14:49.226
It actually requires
very ordinary processes.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:52.518
Just the willingness to give them a go.

00:14:52.542 --> 00:14:55.184
I think we all have moments in life

00:14:55.208 --> 00:14:57.101
where our life path splits

00:14:57.125 --> 00:14:59.476
and the journey we thought
we were going down

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:03.809
veers off to some terrible direction

00:15:03.833 --> 00:15:06.018
that we never anticipated,

00:15:06.042 --> 00:15:08.684
and we certainly didn't want.

00:15:08.708 --> 00:15:09.958
It happened to me.

00:15:10.875 --> 00:15:13.809
It was awful beyond imagining.

00:15:13.833 --> 00:15:18.934
If you ever find yourselves
in a situation where you think

00:15:18.958 --> 00:15:22.184
"There's no way
I'm coming back from this,"

00:15:22.208 --> 00:15:25.434
I urge you to lean into these strategies

00:15:25.458 --> 00:15:27.458
and think again.

00:15:28.958 --> 00:15:31.101
I won't pretend

00:15:31.125 --> 00:15:33.625
that thinking this way is easy.

00:15:34.458 --> 00:15:37.601
And it doesn't remove all the pain.

00:15:37.625 --> 00:15:42.351
But if I've learned anything
over the last five years,

00:15:42.375 --> 00:15:46.101
it is that thinking this way
really does help.

00:15:46.125 --> 00:15:47.726
More than anything,

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:51.726
it has shown me that it is possible

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:55.893
to live and grieve at the same time.

00:15:55.917 --> 00:16:00.059
And for that, I would be always grateful.

00:16:00.083 --> 00:16:01.351
Thank you.

00:16:01.375 --> 00:16:04.417
(Applause)

